%% Live GPU shaders on slides — a small demoscene homage, and how it is made.
%% Build:  python3 lmr2svg.py examples/live-shaders/deck.lmr -o live-shaders.html
%% In the browser: → / Space step the scenes; press m for the soundtrack.
!footer A World Made of Maths

#[.plain] A World Made of Maths
## every pixel you see is computed live on your GPU — press → to wake the sky, m for sound

!shader
	!src aurora.glsl
	!viewport full
	!steps 3
	!sound aurora

!slide[.middle] What am I looking at?

No video and no images: a few hundred lines of **GLSL** inside this deck, a
function evaluated for each of the ~2 million pixels of your screen, sixty
times a second.

!pause

Each keypress moves a scene to its next state — the shader sees an eased
`iStep`, so dawn __glides__ into day. Press **m** for a generative soundtrack.

!pause

:: lemur
	!shader
		!src aurora.glsl
		!viewport full
		!steps 3
		!sound aurora

!slide Mountains from noise

!columns[42 58]
	!column[.center]
		**Fractional Brownian motion**: add octaves of smooth noise, each twice
		as fine and half as strong,

		:: math
			\mathrm{fbm}(p) = \sum_{k=0}^{K-1} 2^{-k}\, n\!\left(2^k R^k p\right)

		One octave per keypress: continents, then hills, then boulders — the
		same statistics at every scale.
	!column
		!shader
			!src fbm.glsl
			!steps 5

!slide Rays that know how far to go

!columns[60 40]
	!column
		!anim
			!src spheretrace.py
	!column[.center]
		A **signed distance function** $f$ says how far the nearest surface is —
		so a ray may safely jump that far:

		:: math
			t_{k+1} = t_k + f\big(o + t_k\,d\big)

		The circles never cross the scene; the steps shrink near a surface and
		stop at $f < \varepsilon$. For terrain, $f \approx p_y - h(p_{xz})$.

!slide[.plain]

!shader
	!src terrain.glsl
	!viewport full
	!steps 3
	!sound drone

!gap[fill]

!style[.center]
	[a homage to __Elevated__ — dawn, day, dusk, night: press →]{#eef3f8}

!slide Clouds are light, lost in fog

!columns[40 60]
	!column[.center]
		Light through a cloud decays as it goes (**Beer–Lambert**),

		:: math
			T = \exp\!\Big(-\!\int \sigma_t(s)\, ds\Big)

		and scatters mostly forward (**Henyey–Greenstein**):

		:: math
			p(\theta) = \frac{1 - g^2}{4\pi\,(1 + g^2 - 2g\cos\theta)^{3/2}}

		Steps: fair weather · cumulus · sunset · a storm.
	!column
		!shader
			!src clouds.glsl
			!steps 3

!slide Fluids without a solver

A velocity field that is the **curl** of a smooth noise stream function cannot
compress — it swirls like a fluid, for free:

:: math
	v = \nabla^{\perp}\psi = \big(\partial_y \psi,\; -\partial_x \psi\big), \qquad \nabla \cdot v = 0

!shader
	!src flow.glsl
	!steps 3

!slide Put a shader on your slide

!columns[48 52]
	!column
		:: lemur
			!shader
				!src flow.glsl
				!viewport body
				!steps 3
				!sound drone
				!quality 0.75

		`#include "common.glsl"` works — lemur inlines it at build time.
	!column
		!table
		!cols Uniform | Meaning
		!noheader

			`iResolution`, `iTime` | Shadertoy's, so snippets paste in
			`iStep`                | the slide step, eased (0 … `!steps`)
			`iStepRaw`             | the same, as an integer
			`iMouse`               | the pointer over the canvas

		`!viewport full` draws behind the slide's text; `!quality` caps the
		resolution (it also adapts to hold the frame rate). Printing keeps the
		last frame.

#[.plain] Thank you
## inspired by __Elevated__ (Rgba & TBC, 2009) — rendered live, as it should be

!shader
	!src finale.glsl
	!viewport full
	!sound aurora
